# Longhorn disk-headroom settings for an EXISTING cluster.
#
# ⚠️  OPERATOR-GATED. Nothing applies this file: not CI, not src/bootstrap.rs,
# not any directory apply. The `.yaml.example` suffix keeps it out of
# `kubectl apply -f deploy/bootstrap/` on purpose. Applying it is a deliberate,
# sequenced act — read the ORDERING section first.
#
# ── When you need this ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# deploy/bootstrap/longhorn.yaml carries safe defaults for installs VeloxSearch
# performs itself. It cannot fix a cluster where Longhorn was installed by
# something else (a Helm release, a distro addon, a previous operator): once
# the `Setting` objects exist, install-time defaults are ignored. Changing live
# values means writing the Setting CRs below.
#
# Check what your cluster actually has:
#
#   kubectl -n longhorn-system get settings.longhorn.io \
#     storage-over-provisioning-percentage \
#     storage-minimal-available-percentage \
#     storage-reserved-percentage-for-default-disk \
#     -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,VALUE:.value
#
# The two that commonly drift loose, and why it bites:
#
#   storage-over-provisioning-percentage — how much thin provisioning Longhorn
#     will schedule against a disk. Raised above 100 (200 is a popular
#     "temporary" fix), it lets the sum of nominal volume sizes exceed real
#     disk. Nothing breaks until the volumes actually grow into their nominal
#     size — then a node fills and replicas on it fault.
#
#   storage-minimal-available-percentage — the free-space floor that stops
#     Longhorn scheduling NEW replicas onto a disk. Lowering it does not protect
#     a disk from growth of replicas ALREADY placed, so it is not the safety net
#     it looks like. A node can pass this check and still run out of space.
#
# Together they produce the failure this file exists to undo: a cluster
# scheduled to ~100% of an inflated ceiling, with the floor too low to react in
# time.
#
# ── ORDERING — this is the part that matters ───────────────────────────────
#
# DO NOT APPLY WHILE A NODE IS ALREADY FULL. Dropping over-provisioning from
# 200 to 100 while more is scheduled than the new ceiling allows marks every
# disk unschedulable at once. It deletes nothing — Longhorn does not evict
# replicas to satisfy a lowered ceiling — but it blocks the replica REBUILDS
# that recovery depends on, which is exactly the wrong moment to lose them.
#
# Correct sequence:
#   1. Give the pressured node headroom first: attach a data disk, or reclaim
#      space (stale snapshots, leaked replica directories) after a per-volume
#      redundancy check.
#   2. Confirm every volume is `healthy` (none `degraded` or `unknown`) and
#      every node is Schedulable:
#        kubectl -n longhorn-system get volumes.longhorn.io \
#          -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,STATE:.status.state,ROBUSTNESS:.status.robustness
#   3. Apply THIS file.
#   4. Re-check: no volume should become `degraded`, no PVC should go Pending.
#
# ROLLBACK — one command per setting; substitute the values you recorded in
# step 0. Takes effect immediately, no restart, no data movement:
#
#   kubectl -n longhorn-system patch settings.longhorn.io \
#     storage-over-provisioning-percentage --type=merge -p '{"value":"200"}'
#   kubectl -n longhorn-system patch settings.longhorn.io \
#     storage-minimal-available-percentage --type=merge -p '{"value":"10"}'
#
# NOTE ON 100%: at 100 the cluster may not have room for every current volume's
# nominal size. That is the honest position — the capacity was never there;
# over-provisioning only hid it. If step 4 leaves PVCs Pending, the answer is
# more disk, not a higher multiplier.
---
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: Setting
metadata: { name: storage-over-provisioning-percentage, namespace: longhorn-system }
value: "100"
---
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: Setting
metadata: { name: storage-minimal-available-percentage, namespace: longhorn-system }
value: "25"
---
# Longhorn's own default; written so the value is recorded rather than assumed.
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: Setting
metadata: { name: storage-reserved-percentage-for-default-disk, namespace: longhorn-system }
value: "30"
